--TEST--
`query` refuses a `tainted` statement and binds a `tainted` value freely, so untrusted data reaches a database only through a placeholder — `rule:security/sink-predicate`, `rule:core-classes/db-one-api` and `rule:core-classes/db-parameters`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/sink-predicate` is the whole injection story for `Core\Db\Connection`: the
// query-text parameter is a **sink**, so a value that came from outside the
// process cannot be written into the statement at all, and a bound parameter
// accepts the same value freely. There is no escaper to launder one with —
// `PDO::quote`, `mysqli_real_escape_string` and `pg_escape_string` have no
// equivalent (`rule:core-classes/db-one-api`), which is only tenable because the second half of
// this case compiles.
tainted string $outside = "ada";

var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");

// The refusal, and nothing about the value is wrong: it is where it is being
// put. This is the line `"select … where owner = '" . $_GET["owner"] . "'"`
// becomes, and it does not reach a server to be found out.
var $refused = $db->query($outside, []);

// The way through, which is the same value at the same statement. These two
// lines contribute no diagnostic, and that is half the claim: a sink with no
// admitting spelling beside it would be a member no program can pass data to.
var $bound = $db->query("select id from accounts where owner = ?", [$outside]);
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:16:27
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
